---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FrontispieceMap from "../components/FrontispieceMap.astro";
---

<Layout title="The frontispiece">
  <header class="mb-2">
    <p class="eyebrow">Appendix</p>
    <h1 class="mt-1 font-serif text-3xl font-semibold">The frontispiece</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-lg text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">
      The most famous title page in philosophy, engraved by Abraham Bosse<sup class="fn-ref"
        ><a id="fnref-1" href="#fn-1">1</a></sup
      > in Paris in 1651 with Hobbes at his
      elbow, and an argument in its own right: every instrument of the civil power on the left has its
      ecclesiastical double on the right, and one figure holds the emblems of both. Hover over any part of the
      plate — or tap it — to see what it shows.
    </p>
  </header>

  <FrontispieceMap />

  <section class="mt-12 max-w-prose">
    <h2 id="on-the-plate" class="section-heading">
      <a href="#on-the-plate" class="heading-anchor">On the plate</a>
    </h2>
    <div class="prose-chapter mt-4 font-serif text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        The design was almost certainly worked out between Hobbes and the engraver rather than delivered to him:
        Bosse was the finest etcher in Paris, Hobbes was living there, and the manuscript copy presented to
        Charles II carries a variant of the same design drawn by hand, with the giant's body made of faces
        looking outward rather than backs turned. Nothing in the book explains the picture; the picture was
        evidently expected to explain the book.
      </p>
      <p>
        It repays the reading. The giant is the <em>Matter</em> and the <em>Forme</em> of the title, the
        landscape below him its <em>Power</em> at work, and the two columns are <em>Ecclesiasticall</em> and
        <em>Civil</em> set against each other panel for panel — fortress and church, coronet and mitre, cannon
        and thunderbolt, arms and distinctions, battle and disputation. The book's claim that these are two
        kinds of the same thing, and must be held by the same hands, is on the cover before it is anywhere in
        the text.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="chapter-footnotes max-w-prose">
    <p class="chapter-footnotes__title" id="notes">Notes</p>
    <ol class="footnotes">
      <li id="fn-1">
        <a href="#fnref-1" class="footnotes__num" aria-label="Back to the text">1</a>
        <span>
          <a class="external" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Bosse" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            ><strong>Abraham Bosse</strong></a
          > (c. 1604&ndash;1676), the leading French printmaker of the century: some 1,600 etchings, a treatise
          that fixed the technique for everyone after him, and a running quarrel with the French academy over
          perspective that got him expelled from it. A Huguenot working for a largely Catholic market, which may
          be why a book holding church and state in one pair of hands did not trouble him. The attribution of
          the <em>Leviathan</em> plate to Bosse is modern &mdash; the engraving is unsigned &mdash; and is owed
          to Noel Malcolm, who also identified the optical device behind the giant's composite body:
          Jean-Fran&ccedil;ois Niceron's multiplying glass, which Hobbes would have seen demonstrated in Paris.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</Layout>
